		"selfHostingGuide": {
			"summary": "What self-hosting involves, with links to the maintained Docker Compose configuration and installation steps.",
			"answer": "To self-host Kaneo, run the application and PostgreSQL with Docker Compose, configure your public URL and secrets, and put a TLS reverse proxy in front. The installation guide specifies at least 2 GB of RAM and 10 GB of disk space. Plan for ongoing updates, database backups, and storage for uploaded files.",
			"requirements": "The documented minimum is 2 GB of RAM and 10 GB of disk space, with Docker 20.10 or newer and Docker Compose V2. Allow more capacity for your workload, database growth, and uploaded files.",
			"compose": "Use the maintained compose.yml in the Docker Compose guide. It defines PostgreSQL, a persistent database volume, the Kaneo application, and health checks. Configure the environment file before starting the services; for production, pin an image version so updates are deliberate.",
			"backups": "Back up both PostgreSQL and any object storage used for uploads. Choose a schedule based on how much work your team can afford to lose, store copies off the application server, and test restoring them before you need them.",
			"worth": "Self-hosting makes sense when you need control over infrastructure and data, and someone can own maintenance. Include server capacity, storage, backups, and that maintenance time when comparing costs with a managed service.",
			"minimum": "The installation guide specifies Docker 20.10 or newer, Docker Compose V2, at least 2 GB of RAM, and 10 GB of disk space. The Compose setup includes PostgreSQL. Redis is optional for a single API instance.",
			"composeLink": "Docker Compose configuration",
			"composeLinkDescription": "The maintained compose.yml with persistent storage and health checks.",
			"startLink": "Start and verify the services",
			"startLinkDescription": "Start the stack, inspect its status, and check that the application is available."
